Unidimensional Dataset Container & Positional Retrieval

series_creation_indexing.cpp

1. Objective & Technical Scope
This module expands on individual Value cells to build the unidimensional sequence container: the Series class. The objective was to couple metadata (column names) with contiguous collections of Value objects, providing fast, positional element indexing.

2. My Implementation Process
In this phase of the codebase, I engineered the following logic:

Constructing the Series Object:

I initialized a Series object named scores by passing a column identifier string ("student_scores") alongside an initializer list of double-precision values: {55.0, 80.5, 92.0, 43.5, 67.0}.

The constructor automatically converts each numeric literal into an encapsulated Value cell and pushes it into an internal std::vector<Value> buffer.

Implementing Position-Based Indexing:

I used the public .at(size_t index) member function to query the element located at index position 0.

Because .at() returns a const Value& reference, I processed the cell through the as_number() helper function to extract the primitive 55.0 double value.

The retrieved score was displayed cleanly via standard stream output (std::cout).

Enforcing Bounds Checking & Encapsulation:

Unlike standard C-style arrays or raw pointer indexing ([]), using .at() enforces boundary checking.

If an out-of-bounds index is passed, the method throws a std::out_of_range exception (inheriting from std::exception), preventing buffer overflows and arbitrary memory access.

3. Verification & Results
Executing series_creation_indexing.cpp confirmed that the first element in the sequence was correctly identified as 55.0. The result proves that positional indexing safely bridges client-side queries with encapsulated internal vector buffers.
